{"id":"8177555d-69fc-42a4-a00c-553559d2845a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785012510,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    required_symbols = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\\n    if not nodes:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No nodes provided\\\")\\n    ids = set()\\n    root_found = None\\n    nodes_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node.get('id')\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        symbol = node.get('symbol')\\n        depth = node.get('depth')\\n        removable = node.get('removable')\\n        utility = node.get('utility')\\n        if nid is None or symbol is None or depth is None or removable is None or utility is None:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required field\\\")\\n        if nid in ids:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate node id {nid}\\\")\\n        ids.add(nid)\\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n        if parent is None:\\n            if root_found is not None:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Multiple roots\\\")\\n            root_found = nid\\n    if root_found is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root node\\\")\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent not in nodes_by_id:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent {parent} not found\\\")\\n            children[parent].append(nid)\\n    all_symbols = {node['symbol'] for node in nodes}\\n    if not required_symbols.issubset(all_symbols):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Required symbols not present in tree\\\")\\n    retained = set(ids)\\n    removed_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_found:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        retained_symbols = {nodes_by_id[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(retained_symbols):\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids according to the described algorithm. The task appears to be a simplified Perses-like tree reduction: given a tree of nodes with parent references, depths, removable flag, utility (though not used explicitly in the description? Only required_symbols matter? The description says \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" It mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", but the provided cases have no grammar rules; only nodes with symbols, parent, depth, removable, utility. The description mentions \"required_symbols\" as in the case payload. The rule: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" and \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So the algorithm: traverse nodes deeper first (higher depth), then consider if node is removable, and if removing it would still keep all required symbols present in the tree (including the node's own symbol if it's required? Actually required_symbols is a set of symbols that must remain present in the tree after reduction. So removing a node might remove that symbol unless another node with same symbol exists elsewhere. The traversal order is deeper nodes before shallower, and tie-breaking by node ID (stable). For each node, if removable and not root and not a required symbol? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node is \"required symbol\" if its symbol is in required_symbols. But careful: there could be multiple nodes with same symbol; the requirement is that after reduction, at least one node with that symbol remains. So a node carrying a required symbol can be removed if there is another node with that symbol in the tree that is not being removed (or will be retained). The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we must check after removal, does the set of symbols in the remaining nodes (including descendants of kept nodes) still include all required symbols. Also \"a node with retained children\" — if a node has children that are retained (i.e., we are not removing them), then we cannot remove that node because it would orphan those children? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose children are also removed? Actually we process in order; we might remove nodes deeper first. So if we have already decided to remove a child, then the parent may become a leaf and can be removed later if criteria met. So the algorithm must be iterative: repeatedly consider nodes in order, and remove if conditions satisfied, updating the tree state (but not mutating input, so we need a working copy). The output is removed_node_ids (ordered removal sequence) and retained_node_ids (final set of node IDs that remain after all removals). Also \"Use deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID (lexicographic or whatever). Then iterate through that sorted list repeatedly? Or just once? Because removal of a node may affect removal eligibility of its parent (now it has no children?). Since we process deeper first, and we might remove a child, then parent may become eligible later. So we might need multiple passes: after each removal, the parent might become a leaf and could be removed in subsequent passes. But the description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests a single pass ordering, but then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" and \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" If we process strictly deeper first, and we remove a child, then when we later process the parent, its children may have been removed, so it has no retained children, so it can be removed if other conditions hold. So a single pass in descending depth order should suffice, because all children have higher depth, so they appear earlier in the list and are considered before the parent. If a child is removed, then when we later consider the parent, its children set is empty (since all were removed), so condition \"retained children\" is false. However, we must be careful: if a child is not removable (e.g., required symbol), then it remains, and that child is retained, so parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. So single pass works. So algorithm:\n\n1. Build a mapping from node id to node object. Also build parent->children adjacency (or simply for each node, we can track its children list from the source data). But the input nodes list does not directly list children; we need to derive from parent field. We'll build a dict nodes_by_id. Then for each node, add to parent's children list if parent is not None.\n2. Determine root node (parent null). Root must never be removed.\n3. Set of required_symbols.\n4. Create a set of current node IDs in the tree (initially all IDs). We'll also track which nodes are \"retained\" as we go, initially all. We'll have a removal list.\n5. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id (string) ascending for tie break.\n6. Iterate over sorted nodes:\n   - Skip if node id not in current_retained (already removed? Actually we won't remove nodes that are already removed because we only process once; but if we later remove a node, we don't revisit it because it's already past in the sorted order? Since we remove later in the order, but the removal will affect subsequent nodes? Actually if we remove a node, it might affect later nodes (parents) that have lower depth, which appear later in the sorted list. So we need to update the state (current_retained, children sets) as we go. Since we process deeper first, removal of a child doesn't affect any future node that we've already processed (because all children are deeper, already processed). So we can safely remove and update.\n   - Check conditions:\n     a. Node is not root.\n     b. Node is removable (node['removable'] is True).\n     c. Node's symbol is not required? Actually we don't directly check if node itself is required symbol; we need to check that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the retained tree. This is a more global condition: find all retained nodes after this removal, see if the set of symbols contains all required symbols. If yes, allow removal.\n     d. Node has no retained children. \"retained children\" means children that are still in current_retained set. So we need to check children list (from original adjacency, but updated for removals). Since we only remove nodes, children lists remain valid: if a child was removed, its id is not in retained set, so it's not \"retained child\". So condition: for each child id in children_of_node, if child id is in current_retained, then node has retained children -> cannot remove.\n   - If all conditions satisfied:\n     - Add node id to removed_node_ids list.\n     - Remove node id from current_retained set.\n     - Optionally, update root's children? Not needed because root is never removed.\n7. After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids = sorted list of current_retained set (the problem says \"sorted retained IDs\" in description, so sort alphabetically/numerically? likely string sort). Return object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nEdge cases: The case payload has nodes array. The root always has id \"root\" and depth 0. required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". The symbol \"root\" is only at root, so root cannot be removed because it's the only node with that symbol. Also, required_symbols[\"s1\"]: there are multiple nodes with s1 (n1 and n4 in first case). n1 is depth 1, not removable (removable=false). n4 is depth 2, removable=true. So n4 can be removed because n1 still provides s1. Also n2 is removable, but it's parent of n4 (which might be removed later). Process order by depth descending: depth 2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (n5 is not removable, n6 removable). For first case: n4 (removable true, not root, has children? n4 has no children (since no node has parent=n4). So check required symbols after removal: currently all required symbols present (root, s1). After removing n4, s1 still present via n1. So remove n4. n5 not removable. n6 removable, has no children, not root, check required symbols: after removal, s1 still present (n1), s0 is not required but s0 is symbol of n6; root is there. So remove n6. Then depth 1 nodes: n1 not removable, n2 removable, n3 not removable. At this point, current_retained includes root, n1, n2, n3, n5. n2 has children? original children of n2 is n4. n4 removed, so no retained children. Check required symbols: after removing n2, all required symbols present: root and s1 (n1). So remove n2. Then n3 not removable. Root not removable. So final retained: root, n1, n3, n5. removed order: n4, n6, n2. retained sorted: n1, n3, n5, root (alphabetically: n1, n3, n5, root). Wait root comes last alphabetically because 'r' > 'n'? Actually 'root' vs 'n...': 'n' < 'r', so root after n5? Actually 'root' starts with 'r', 'n' < 'r', so order: n1, n3, n5, root. That matches typical string sort. But check description: \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - likely ascending string order. That is fine.\n\nNow need to ensure that we don't remove required_symbols entirely. Condition check: after removal, compute set of symbols from all retained nodes. Must include all required.\n\nImplementation details: We'll copy the nodes list into a list of dicts (or use references, but careful not to mutate input). Build adjacency: children = {id: [] for all nodes}. For each node, if parent, add node id to parent's children list. Also we need to quickly get symbol for a node id.\n\nWe'll maintain retained set as set of ids initially all. Then sorted order: sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])). Iterate, check conditions, if pass, add to removal list and remove from retained set.\n\nComplexity fine.\n\nNow output: {\"removed_node_ids\": list, \"retained_node_ids\": list}. The list order for removed_node_ids is the order of removal (which matches the iteration order). The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids in removal order, retained_node_ids sorted.\n\nNow we need to embed this in Python code inside a JSON response with key \"implementation\". The code must define function solve(case) that takes the payload (a dict) and returns the result dict.\n\nWe'll write the code accordingly.\n\nCheck for potential issues: The case may have nodes that are not in a tree? But assume it's valid tree. The root might have a symbol not \"root\"? In the fixtured cases, root symbol is \"root\". But we don't hardcode.\n\nAlso note: The condition \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" But we are already checking required symbols via global requirement. However, what if a node's symbol is required but there are multiple instances? Then we can remove it if another remains. So the \"Never remove a required symbol\" is actually a condition that we should not remove a node if its symbol is required AND it is the only node with that symbol? But in the description it says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That seems to say never remove a node that itself has a required symbol. But that would conflict with n4 (symbol s1) being removed. In the fixture, n4 is removed despite being s1. So the true interpretation is: \"Never remove a node if after removal, the required symbols would be missing.\" The \"required symbol\" check is per-node? Actually the description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That might be a misstatement or simplified; the later part says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That overrides the per-node \"required symbol\" rule. So we follow the global check. We'll ignore the \"Never remove a required symbol\" and use the global required_symbols condition.\n\nBut careful: The description also says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" and \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields. Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should perform input validation: check that 'nodes' is a list, each node has required fields: 'id', 'parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility' (though utility not used). Also required_symbols must be present. But for now, we assume valid.\n\nRaise ValueError if infeasible: for example, if a required symbol is not present in any node at all, then no removal possible? But we should still run? The algorithm would at least retain all nodes because removal would fail global check? But if initial set lacks a required symbol, it's invalid. So we should check that initially all required symbols appear in the nodes. Also check that there is exactly one root (parent null). Raise ValueError if not satisfied.\n\nWe'll include validation at start.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\nThe response must be a JSON object with single key \"implementation\" containing a JSON-escaped string of the Python code.\n\nWe'll write code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\n    required_symbols = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\n    if not nodes:\n        raise ValueError(\"No nodes provided\")\n    # Validate nodes\n    ids = set()\n    root_found = None\n    nodes_by_id = {}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node.get('id')\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        symbol = node.get('symbol')\n        depth = node.get('depth')\n        removable = node.get('removable')\n        utility = node.get('utility')\n        if nid is None or symbol is None or depth is None or removable is None or utility is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"Node missing required field\")\n        if nid in ids:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate node id {nid}\")\n        ids.add(nid)\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = node\n        children[nid] = []  # initialize\n        if parent is None:\n            if root_found is not None:\n                raise ValueError(\"Multiple roots\")\n            root_found = nid\n        else:\n            # parent will be added later after all nodes are registered\n            pass\n    if root_found is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root node\")\n    # Build children\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        parent = node['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            if parent not in nodes_by_id:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {parent} not found\")\n            children[parent].append(nid)\n    # Check required symbols present initially\n    all_symbols = {node['symbol'] for node in nodes}\n    if not required_symbols.issubset(all_symbols):\n        raise ValueError(\"Required symbols not present in tree\")\n    # Initialize retained set\n    retained = set(ids)\n    removed_order = []\n    # Sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending for ties\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        # Skip if already removed (should not happen)\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Root never removed\n        if nid == root_found:\n            continue\n        # Check removable\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check if node has retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Simulate removal: compute symbols after removal\n        # Note: we must check if all required symbols remain\n        # Temporarily remove and check\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        # Compute symbols in retained set\n        retained_symbols = {nodes_by_id[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\n        if required_symbols.issubset(retained_symbols):\n            # Valid removal\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n            # already removed from retained set\n        else:\n            # Revert\n            retained.add(nid)\n    # After loop, retained set is final\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)  # string sort\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nMake sure to escape properly in JSON string.\n\nNow output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4331,"total_tokens":6500,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3693},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}